<p>Cold Turkey Blocker is a useful Freedom alternative for strict Windows or Mac sessions without a recurring Pro subscription. FocusMe suits configurable plans, while SelfControl is a free Mac option for timed website blocking. Consider Opal for phone-focused rules, one sec for a pause before opening distracting apps, or Apple Screen Time for built-in limits. These tools do not all replace Freedom's coordinated multi-device workflow. Freedom itself now has a real free tier, so check whether that already meets your needs. Choose by your devices, the interruption you want, and what must remain accessible during a session.</p>
<h2>What Freedom already does, including its free tier</h2>
<p><a href="https://freedom.to/">Freedom</a> blocks distracting apps, websites, and internet access across supported devices. Its central appeal is coordinating a focus session rather than configuring an unrelated timer on every screen. If your distraction moves from a laptop to a phone, that coordination can be the feature worth keeping.</p>
<p>The current <a href="https://support.freedom.to/en/articles/13764747-what-s-included-in-free-and-premium-plans">Free and Premium guide</a> lists a genuine free account tier. It includes Start Now sessions, custom blocklists, and unlimited devices. Free sessions cannot exceed two hours; recurring sessions, Start Later, and Locked Mode require Premium. This matters because older comparison pages describe Freedom as only a limited trial.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://freedom.to/premium">pricing page</a> lists Premium at US$8.99 month to month and shows US$3.33 per month under the yearly choice. The latter is an annual-plan equivalent, not a US$3.33 rolling monthly subscription; confirm the annual charge at checkout. The page also advertises a discounted US$99.50 lifetime offer at the time checked. Offers, local taxes, and checkout totals can change.</p>
<p>Keep Freedom if the free limits are enough or if its coordinated paid schedule already solves the problem. Switch for a specific reason: a desktop-only one-time purchase, a more detailed plan, a different phone interaction, or an included operating-system feature. There is no need to replace it merely because another list calls something a productivity winner.</p>
<h2>Locking is a setting, not a universal guarantee</h2>
<p>Freedom's <a href="https://support.freedom.to/en/articles/1802927-locked-mode">Locked Mode guide</a> describes settings on the dashboard and on devices separately. The dashboard limits End Session to once in seven days when locked, and inactive upcoming sessions remain editable. On iOS, the guide explains that protecting Freedom's Screen Time permission requires a separate Screen Time passcode. These details are more useful than saying a session is impossible to escape.</p>
<p>Different alternatives make different tradeoffs between a timer, password, waiting period, and permission protection. Choose the level that fits your work and test it on a short session before committing to a long one. Required communications, authentication, and work resources should be accounted for in the blocklist you choose.</p>
